{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 16,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.648,
            0.047,
            0.846,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Working with Fractions"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.087,
            0.848,
            0.126
          ],
          "content": "2. We then multiply the dividend with this reciprocal to get the quotient."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.13,
            0.566,
            0.151
          ],
          "content": "Summarising, to divide two fractions:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.154,
            0.547,
            0.174
          ],
          "content": "Find the reciprocal of the divisor"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.186,
            0.179,
            0.695,
            0.199
          ],
          "content": "• Multiply this by the dividend to get the quotient."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.217,
            0.219,
            0.253,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": "\\mathrm { S o } ,",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_5.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.403,
            0.209,
            0.6,
            0.247
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{a}{b} \\div \\frac{c}{d} = \\frac{d}{c} \\times \\frac{a}{b} = \\frac{d \\times a}{c \\times b}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.253,
            0.426,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": "This can be rewritten as:"
        },
        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.448,
            0.97,
            0.551,
            0.985
          ],
          "content": "Reprint 2026-27"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.398,
            0.284,
            0.597,
            0.321
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{a}{b} \\div \\frac{c}{d} = \\frac{a}{b} \\times \\frac{d}{c} = \\frac{a \\times d}{b \\times c}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_9.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.328,
            0.848,
            0.404
          ],
          "content": "As with methods and formulas for addition, subtraction, and multiplication of fractions that you learnt earlier, this method and formula for division of fractions, in this general form, was first explicitly stated by Brahmagupta in his Brāhmasphuṭasiddhānta (628 CE)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.406,
            0.846,
            0.461
          ],
          "content": "So, to evaluate, for example, $\\frac{2}{3} \\div \\frac{3}{5}$ using Brahmagupta’s formula above, we write:"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.47,
            0.415,
            0.509
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{2}{3} \\div \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{2}{3} \\times \\frac{5}{3} = \\frac{2 \\times 5}{3 \\times 3} = \\frac{10}{9}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_12.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.53,
            0.586,
            0.553
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Dividend, Divisor and the Quotient"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.561,
            0.847,
            0.6
          ],
          "content": "When we divide two whole numbers, say $6 \\div 3 ,$ , we get the quotient 2. Here the quotient is less than the dividend."
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.426,
            0.608,
            0.573,
            0.63
          ],
          "content": "6 \\div 3 = 2, 2 < 6",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.638,
            0.577,
            0.67
          ],
          "content": "But what happens when we divide 6 by $\\frac { 1 } { 4 }$ ?"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.446,
            0.676,
            0.55,
            0.711
          ],
          "content": "6 \\div \\frac{1}{4} = 24.",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_17.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.714,
            0.617,
            0.735
          ],
          "content": "Here the quotient is greater than the dividend!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.736,
            0.543,
            0.769
          ],
          "content": "What happens when we divide $\\frac { 1 } { 8 } \\mathrm { b y }   \\frac { 1 } { 4 }   ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.452,
            0.775,
            0.546,
            0.811
          ],
          "content": "{ \\frac { 1 } { 8 } } \\div { \\frac { 1 } { 4 } } = { \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } .",
          "image_source": "images/page_16_equation_20.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.814,
            0.653,
            0.832
          ],
          "content": "Here too the quotient is greater than the dividend."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.832,
            0.848,
            0.869
          ],
          "content": "When do you think the quotient is less than the dividend and when is it greater than the dividend?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.869,
            0.848,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": "Is there a similar relationship between the divisor and the quotient?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.808,
            0.938,
            0.846,
            0.955
          ],
          "content": ""
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
